  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery currently shows dry\nweather across east central Florida. GOES-19 satellite imagery and\nlocal observations indicate partly to mostly cloudy skies across\neast central Florida. Temperatures are currently in the mid 80s to\nlow 90s with dew points in the mid 70s to around 80 degrees. Winds\nare breezy from the southeast at 10-15 mph with gusts to 20-28mph.\nThe 10Z XMR sounding showed MLCAPE at 1,327 J/kg, 0-6km shear at\n31kts, a PWAT value at 2.11,\" and a 500mb temperature at -6.4C.\nAnalysis charts show the Atlantic ridge extended over the Florida\nPanhandle with Major Hurricane Isaias moving north-northeast at\n16mph near the Alabama coast.\n\nScattered to numerous (~30-70% chance) generally north-northeast \nmoving showers and lightning storms are forecast this afternoon and \nevening before chances gradually diminish around 8PM. Guidance \nshows MLCAPE increasing to up to 2,500 J/kg, 0-6km shear up to\n35kts, a LLJ up to 25-30kts across northern ECFL, and PWAT values\nup to around 2.3-2.5\" (90th percentile to the maximum percentile \nfor the date). The aforementioned weather parameters are\nsufficient for isolated strong storms capable of occasional to \nfrequent lighting, wind gusts up to 35-45mph, and heavy rainfall \n(up to 1-2\" in 60minutes). Expect breezy southeast winds to \ngradually weaken tonight at around 5-10mph. Above normal (~4F-8F \ndegrees) low temperatures in the low to upper 70s are expected. \n\nThe Weekend... The wet weather pattern is expected to continue\nthrough the weekend as Major Hurricane Isaias transitions to a\nextratropical system with a cold front - extending from the low \nover central Tennessee - across east central Florida on Sunday. \nRain chances (~50-80% chance) will remain high through the weekend\nwith scattered to numerous showers and lightning storms expected \neach afternoon and evening hours. However, the coverage of showers\nand lightning storms will be nearly equal across all of east \ncentral Florida on Saturday; then the coverage will be greatest across\nareas to the south of I-4 on Sunday as the aforementioned cold \nfront moves south-southeast across east central Florida. Guidance\nshows moderate instability, moderate bulk shear values, relatively\nwarm 500mb temperatures at -5C to -6C, and PWAT values up to\n2.3-2.5\" (90th percentile to the maximum percentile \nfor the date). Thus, the primary hazards with isolated strong\nstorms will be occasional to frequent lightning, wind gusts up to\n40-50mph, and heavy rainfall (up to 1-3\"+ in 60 minutes).\nAdditionally, there is a Marginal Risk (level 1 out of 5) for \nexcessive rainfall on Saturday.\n\nMonday-Thursday... Deterministic models and LREF-clusters are in\ngood agreement that the Atlantic ridge will weaken and shift east\nover the western Atlantic as a longwave trough deepens over the\neastern CONUS/western Atlantic Monday into Tuesday. The\naforementioned guidance then shows a mid/upper level ridge\nbuilding over the eastern CONUS on Thursday; although there is\ndisagreement on how far north the ridge builds and for how long it\nstays over the eastern CONUS. Much drier air filters in across\neast central Florida with mostly dry conditions across the region\nthrough Thursday. However, isolated showers cannot be ruled out\nacross the Treasure Coast Monday and Thursday. Expect offshore \nflow through Tuesday before flow shifts onshore\nmidweek. Seasonable high temperatures are forecast with seasonable\nto above normal low temperatures.",
